Here is a practice question at the same level as the previous one. It tests your understanding of how Java chooses between a reference type and an actual object type when dealing with variables and methods.
Try to solve this without running it in JShell first:
class Vehicle {
    String model = "Generic Vehicle";
    
    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Driving a " + model);
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    String model = "Luxury Sedan";
    
    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Driving a " + model);
    }
}

public class PracticeMain {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Vehicle v = new Car();
        
        System.out.print(v.model + " -> ");
        v.drive();
    }
}

What is the output?
A) Generic Vehicle -> Driving a Generic Vehicle
B) Luxury Sedan -> Driving a Luxury Sedan
C) Generic Vehicle -> Driving a Luxury Sedan
D) Luxury Sedan -> Driving a Generic Vehicle
